—With the unexpected intrusion of Tīga Laureon and Pūka, the battle's conclusion had been thoroughly muddled.

Honestly, all Subaru could say was that Tīga had completely outplayed them.

Regardless of whether the information they'd obtained was trustworthy, if they'd managed to secure the two defeated enemies, they could have gotten at least a little closer to uncovering the secrets of the mysterious Beloved Children.

Even if that hadn't been possible, they would at least have succeeded in reducing the enemy's already troublesome fighting strength.

But all of that had gone up in smoke when their captives were taken back.

“Still, if the battle had continued, we'd probably have been the ones in trouble. I never imagined Tīga was a Spirit Arts user. He really had me fooled.”

“—No. That's not it. Tīga isn't a Spirit Arts user. He should simply be together with Pūka, that's all…”

“You mean without a contract? Like Anastasia and Eridona? …If that's the case, then what exactly is the relationship between those two?”

“That's… I'm sorry. I don't know. All I know is that they aren't bound by a contract.”

Melty's answer was vague as she gripped her contracted shooting staff, now reduced in size, and lowered her head.

However, Subaru didn't think her vagueness came from lying or trying to conceal something.

Most likely, Melty genuinely didn't know the details of the relationship between Tīga and Pūka.

But things should have been different when it came to her own relationship with Tīga.

“—I'll definitely tell you everything later. But right now…”

“I know. Checking the situation in Kugelschreiber Castle and reuniting with Beako and the others takes priority… Ferris! What about your arms?! Are they going to reattach properly?!”

“I'm concentrating, so stop talking to me! I'm numbing the pain, and you're making my hands shake!”

“Your hands are shaking? Your hands are literally detached…”

Ferris snapped back in his high-pitched voice.

He was in the middle of reattaching his own arms—the portions from his elbows down that had been cut off and pinned to the wall—using healing magic.

Even Subaru hadn't expected things to go as far as having Ferris's arms severed. If they couldn't be restored, Subaru would have to spend the rest of his life making amends to him.

When he'd said something along those lines, Ferris had given him another expression of utter disgust.

“Besides, if someone were to spend their whole life atoning for this, wouldn't it be the person who actually cut them off? Namely, me.”

“I do feel pretty responsible for making you do that, though. What about you, Miss Crusch? Are you okay physically?”

“I have no serious injuries. I can still—”

She had presumably intended to say that she could still fight.

But contrary to her determination to answer that way, her body couldn't keep up with her resolve.

—She suddenly staggered, her knees giving way.

Subaru immediately reached out and hurriedly caught her as she began to fall.

“Whoa! Careful! Are you okay?”

“Ah… Subaru… sama…”

“It must be a side effect of the Dragon's Eye. Miss Crusch, you've been pushing yourself incredibly hard.”

“But I can still…”

“No. Don't push yourself. Get some rest for now. We'll make up for whatever we're lacking. Mainly Black Dog, though.”

Black Dog responded indignantly with a “Whaaat?” at Subaru's answer as he looked down at the feebly stirring Crusch, but Subaru silenced him with a glare.

Subaru then took the eyepatch from the pocket of Crusch's coat and gently placed it over her left eye, whose eyelid was growing heavy.

“We'll talk again when you wake up. About the story of how hard Miss Crusch worked and all that.”

“—Yes.”

Crusch nodded as her consciousness faded, then closed her remaining visible eye as well.

Over these past two months, whenever she'd been forced to rely on the power of the Dragon's Eye and exhausted herself into sleep, she'd always seemed stern and tense, as though she were constantly on guard.

Now, however, Subaru thought that the tension had left her sleeping face.

That sight brought him a small measure of relief.

“—Subaru-kun.”

As Subaru adjusted his hold on the sleeping Crusch, Ferris came to stand in front of him.

From the sleeves of his black suit, the arms he'd lost now extended once more. He opened and closed his palms, seemingly showing off how completely they'd healed.

Seeing that they'd been properly reattached, Subaru felt relieved about that, too.

“Honestly, it was so gruesome that I couldn't bear to look directly at it… but you can really heal them even after that?”

“Mm-hmm. As long as the missing body part is still around and the person's od hasn't forgotten what its original state was like, I think I can heal it pretty much perfectly.”

“I feel like you just casually said something absolutely ridiculous, but whatever. I'm just glad. …Is it okay if I carry Miss Crusch? I've got the most free hands right now.”

“—Mm, thanks. I hate you.”

“A tsundere cross-dressing boy is a whole new experience…”

It seemed that Subaru's plan to rely on Ferris had significantly lowered his affection for him.

Considering that Ferris's opinion of Subaru had probably been low to begin with, Subaru suspected it had now plummeted deep into negative territory. He was almost afraid to ask.

Even so, without Ferris—and without Crusch—they would never have been able to repel the Beloved Children.

There were all sorts of lingering emotional complications, but the outcome was something they'd wrested from the enemy by having everyone do their very best.

“Even if Tīga ultimately managed to ruin our victory at the last second…!”

“U-Um, I'm sorry. I'll apologize on Tīga's behalf…”

“Hah! What's with the goody-two-shoes act, apologizing on someone else's behalf?! As if that could ever fill the emptiness and loneliness! Or maybe it could, but it damn well shouldn't! Anyway, what do we do now?”

“First, stop pestering Melty. After that, as I said before—we prioritize reuniting with Beako and the others. But we'll proceed carefully.”

Tīga had retreated with the wounded Beloved Children, but they still had no idea where the third, as-yet-unseen member was located. It was entirely possible that other enemies were lying in wait inside the castle.

They knew far too little about this place to let their guard down and assume they'd driven off every enemy.

Especially now that the castle had emerged into the surface world, high above the clouds along the Lifauss Highway, attracting everyone's attention.

“After all, they say this castle was made by Echidna. Knowing her, she might come up with some trial where we have to escape before this flying castle crashes to the ground, just to test challengers who've made their way inside.”

“What kind of impression do you have of her? She's Beatrice's mother, isn't she?”

“…A twisted personality, just barely short of qualifying as a Sin Archbishop?”

Strictly speaking, in terms of how nasty she was, there wasn't much difference between her and a Sin Archbishop.

However, she'd already passed away, and at least she couldn't cause any further damage. That was the basis for Subaru's assessment.

Still, there was also the matter of Sphinx, who had wreaked havoc in the Vollachian Empire.

Depending on what sins remained hidden, Echidna's reputation could still plummet further.

Looking around at his companions, each of whom wore an unhappy expression at his answer, Subaru continued.

“Anyway, we need to be extremely careful with the floors and walls. Now that we've left the subspace, I doubt we're going to fall endlessly until we die, but…”

“—Ah, well, there's no need to worry quite that much.”

“————”

At the unexpected sound of that voice, Subaru whipped around.

At this point, he really didn't want to deal with yet another unexpected intruder, but his apprehension and anxiety proved unnecessary.

Standing in the stairwell connecting the underground area to the upper floors, peeking his head out from the doorway, was a familiar face—

“—Rachins! You're safe!”

“Don't go casually calling me by my name like that, is what I'd like to say, but… damn, you guys are beat to hell. You look even worse than the Librarian told me.”

The one who spoke as he surveyed Subaru and the others gathered in the underground area was Rachins, who had gone off on his own before their encounter with the Beloved Children.

Unlike Subaru's group, who'd been through one fierce battle after another, Rachins didn't appear to have been caught up in any violent encounters.

Apparently, he'd been lucky enough to avoid running into the enemy.

But—

“Mr. Russell… what about the Silver Fox? Weren't you together?”

“Safe, I guess. Or rather, that bastard's judgment apparently kept us from being discovered by the enemy. We were heading back to the entrance with the documents when the entrance's value suddenly plummeted, or some shit like that…”

“Value? You mean the Divine Protection of Appraisal? Didn't know it could be used like that…”

What Rachins was describing was a sudden collapse in the value of the castle's entrance.

In other words, Russell, as the holder of that Divine Protection, had sensed that the entrance had suddenly changed from something useful to something harmful.

If he'd been able to sense the disturbance at that point, then it made sense that he might have been able to avoid the Beloved Children.

Honestly, Subaru wished they'd been warned, but since they'd been operating separately, there wasn't much that could have been done.

He'd have to be grateful that nothing irreversible had happened to them while he and the others were elsewhere.

“There's a lot I'd like to catch up on, but Long Tongue-kun, things are pretty hectic outside, aren't they?”

“Oh, right! Apparently, the castle flew out of its subspace. It might be a Witch's trap designed to catch all the intruders at once. We need to get out right away…”

“That's not it. The Librarian is the one making it fly, and the Silver Fox is the one who gave the order.”

“—Beako and Mr. Russell?”

Stopped in the middle of his hurried response, Subaru widened his eyes.

It was an unexpected report that Beatrice and Russell were working together on something.

And the fact that what they were doing involved something as spectacular—and completely lacking in secrecy—as making the castle fly made it all the more surprising.

Based on Subaru's experience working as a collaborator of the Six Tongues over the past two months, it should have been self-evident that Russell hated attracting public attention more than anything.

“Both of them are safe, right? What the hell are they thinking, making the castle fly? And what were you doing? Why didn't you stop them?”

“Don't come at me with a whole barrage of questions at once! You're noisy as hell! If you're that curious, then instead of standing around here talking, why don't you just go ask the people involved yourself?”

“Fair enough. What about Door Crossing?”

“I can do it. That's how I got here, too. Damn, what a creepy spell.”

Rachins offered an opinion that would probably make Beatrice puff out her cheeks and stomp her feet if she heard it, then started walking as though to lead Subaru and the others.

After taking his first few steps, however, he stopped.

“Oh, right, right.”

Turning his head back toward them, Rachins added,

“To answer one of your earlier questions, at least… they're going to turn this castle into a base.”

“Huh?”

“That's the answer to your question about why they'd go to the trouble of making the castle fly. They're establishing a base here. —If we're going to seriously reclaim the kingdom, we can't keep running around from place to place, hiding forever.”

And so Rachins explained why Russell, despite his hatred of flashy displays, had nevertheless decided to make the castle fly.


